title: how I described it on Substack
pid: f212e61c-bec4-47ee-9bcc-735a0636ec8a
body: |2-
  I learned a new analogy that seems worth sharing.

  First the old one: What artificial superintelligence should mean to humans is often compared to what humans meant to every other species on Earth. We genuinely *like* most of those species, but we consider ourselves so much smarter and more important that we don't worry too much about destroying them en masse when it's convenient. AI is made in our image.

  The new one: Hernán Cortés conquered Mesoamerica, with a opulation in the millions, with a team of a few hundred solider-sailor-pirates. Their guns weren't much use in battle, but the spectacle did inspire awe, which was helpful. The Spaniards exploited divisions within the American population, but the Spaniards were themselves extremely divided, with sometimes literal fighting between Spanish forces. European diseases did lay waste to American populations, but even after that the numerical advantage of the Americans would have looked overwhellming.

  The critical difference seems to have been a rather (from the modern perspective) trivial minor technological advantage: The Spanish had steel swords and horses.

  The analogy is Dwarkesh Patel's, from his recent interview with Si Sheppard.
